dsh: reasoning:
False FAILs! The failure cause: ledger titles use `C^0` while fetched arXiv titles use `$C^0$` (LaTeX `$...$`). My `norm_text` strips `$...$` math entirely — good — but the ledger title has `C^0` without dollar signs; norm_text turns `C^0` into `c 0` (since `^` is non-alphanumeric → space) while fetched title `$C^0$-inextendibility` → after removing `$...$` becomes ` -inextendibility` (the math content is dropped!). That breaks the match: fetched loses "C^0" while ledger keeps "c 0".

Two fixes:
1. In norm_text, don't drop math content entirely: replace `$...$` delimiters with spaces but keep inner content. Currently `re.sub(r"\$[^$]*\$", " ", s)` removes content. Change to `s = s.replace('$',' ')` (strip only the delimiters).
2. Handle `^` and `_` and braces: `C^{0,1}_{\mathrm{loc}}` → remove LaTeX commands `\mathrm{loc}` → "loc"; braces removed. Current norm removes backslashes only (`\` → space), so `\mathrm{loc}` → " mathrm loc" — noise but tolerable if we drop `\mathrm` command names. Better: replace `\mathrm`, `\text`, `\rm` etc. with space first, then strip `{}`, `^`, `_` to spaces.

A cleaner normalization for titles:
```python
def norm_text(s):
    s = html.unescape(s or "")
    s = s.replace("$", " ")
    s = re.sub(r"\\(?:mathrm|text|rm|bf|it|mathcal|mathbb|operatorname)\s*", " ", s)
    s = s.replace("\\", " ")
    s = re.sub(r"[^0-9A-Za-z]+", " ", s)
    return re.sub(r"\s+", " ", s).strip().lower()
```
So `C^0` → `c 0`; `$C^0$` → `c 0`; `C^{0,1}_{\mathrm{loc}}` → `c 0 1 loc`; ledger `C^{0,1}_loc` → `c 0 1 loc`. 

